{
  "technical_and_design_decisions": [
    "Target the existing Spring XD codebase (Spring Framework 6 / Java 17+ baseline per specification); all changes are made on top of the current admin server, shell, DSL parser, and MessageBus SPI.",
    "U1/U2: Extend the MessageBus SPI with explicit resource lifecycle operations (create/delete/cleanup) and implement them for each supported transport (RabbitMQ queues/exchanges, Kafka topics, Redis keys/channels, in-memory local bus). Stream destroy in the admin server will invoke bus cleanup after module undeployment.",
    "U2: Stale-resource detection relies on the established stream/module naming convention plus persisted stream metadata; a new admin/shell cleanup command enumerates bus resources and removes those not matching any existing stream. Kafka topic deletion is best-effort and logged because broker-side deletion may be disabled or asynchronous.",
    "U3: Stream editing is exposed via a new REST PUT endpoint and a corresponding shell command. Editing an undeployed stream only updates the stored definition/properties; editing a deployed stream updates the definition, then redeploys only the affected modules via the existing deployment state machine.",
    "U4: Deployment manifest files are accepted in Java .properties (key=value) format via a new shell option (e.g., --propertiesFile); file contents are merged with inline properties, with inline values taking precedence, and validated before deployment.",
    "U6: Trace is modeled as a per-module deployment property (module.<name>.trace and module.*.trace wildcard). Implementation uses Spring Integration global wiretap configuration to attach a logging tap channel to the input/output channels of traced modules.",
    "U7: Side channels are declared by modules through convention-based channel metadata; the bus binding step scans module contexts and binds any additional declared channels beyond input/output to the message bus using the stream naming convention.",
    "U5/U8: Reference architectures and the NYC taxi trip analytics solution are delivered as documentation and runnable sample modules/streams in the samples repository, not as changes to the XD runtime core.",
    "U8: The taxi sample ingests raw trip report CSV over an HTTP source stream, parses/enriches records (geo-grid mapping), and computes sliding-window analytics (top routes, busy areas, revenue) using XD stream topologies, with a simple results sink and documentation."
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "A single developer performs all work, including design, implementation, testing, and documentation; no peer review, QA, or PM effort from other people is included.",
    "Estimates represent effort, not elapsed time; 1 workday = 8 productive hours.",
    "The Spring XD source repository, build system, and CI environment already exist and are usable; environment setup effort is excluded.",
    "The developer is already experienced with Spring Framework, Spring Integration, Spring XD internals, RabbitMQ, Kafka, and Redis; no learning/training time is included except modest research for U5 and U8.",
    "Message buses in scope for U1/U2 are RabbitMQ, Kafka, Redis, and the embedded local bus.",
    "U5's scope is limited (per the story) to capturing raw domain-specific reference architecture ideas as a first-step documentation deliverable, not fully validated production architectures.",
    "U8 uses publicly available NYC taxi trip data (DEBS 2015 Grand Challenge) and is delivered as a functional sample sufficient for evaluation, not a competition-optimized submission.",
    "Admin web UI changes are out of scope; new capabilities are exposed via the REST API and the XD shell only.",
    "Testing scope includes unit and integration tests; only lightweight performance validation is done for the U8 sample.",
    "Defect-fixing buffer for issues discovered during the developer's own testing is included in the integration testing activity."
  ],
  "activities": [
    {
      "activity_id": "A1",
      "activity_name": "Increment-level architecture and technical design",
      "activity_description": "Overall design for the increment: MessageBus SPI lifecycle extensions, deployment property model changes (trace, side channels), REST/shell API additions for stream edit and manifest files, naming-convention-based stale resource detection, and test strategy. Produces design notes reviewed against all user stories.",
      "most_likely_effort": 5
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A2",
      "activity_name": "U1 - Message bus resource cleanup on stream destroy",
      "activity_description": "Implement bus resource cleanup so queues/topics/keys created for a stream are deleted when the stream is destroyed. Includes MessageBus SPI additions, per-transport implementations (RabbitMQ, Kafka, Redis, local), integration into the admin server stream-destroy workflow, and unit/integration tests per transport.",
      "most_likely_effort": 8
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A3",
      "activity_name": "U2 - Stale queue/topic cleanup command",
      "activity_description": "Implement detection and removal of orphaned bus resources: enumeration of bus resources by naming convention, comparison against existing streams, a new admin REST endpoint and shell cleanup command, safeguards against deleting active resources, and tests including simulated stale resources.",
      "most_likely_effort": 6
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A4",
      "activity_name": "U3 - Edit deployed/undeployed streams without destroy",
      "activity_description": "Implement stream editing: new REST PUT endpoint and shell command, validation of updated definitions/deployment properties, persistence of changes, selective redeployment of affected modules for deployed streams, error handling for invalid edits, and unit/integration tests.",
      "most_likely_effort": 8
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A5",
      "activity_name": "U4 - Deployment manifest from file",
      "activity_description": "Add shell support for supplying deployment properties from a file (e.g., --propertiesFile), parsing and validating .properties content, merge semantics with inline properties, error reporting for malformed files, tests, and shell help/docs update.",
      "most_likely_effort": 4
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A6",
      "activity_name": "U6 - Trace deployment property with wildcard wiretap",
      "activity_description": "Implement per-module tracing via deployment properties (module.<name>.trace and module.*.trace wildcard): property parsing/resolution against stream modules, Spring Integration global wiretap configuration attaching a logging tap to traced module channels, integration with module deployment, and unit/integration tests verifying traced vs untraced modules.",
      "most_likely_effort": 7
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A7",
      "activity_name": "U7 - Arbitrary side channel binding to the bus",
      "activity_description": "Implement support for module-declared side channels beyond input/output: channel declaration metadata, bus binding of additional channels using stream naming conventions, per-transport behavior verification, DSL/config surface for declaring side channels, and unit/integration tests including an in-flow tap channel scenario.",
      "most_likely_effort": 8
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A8",
      "activity_name": "U5 - Reference architecture documentation on Spring XD",
      "activity_description": "Research and capture raw domain-specific reference architecture ideas (e.g., IoT ingestion, retail clickstream, telco CDR, financial fraud) built on Spring XD: produce structured documentation with stream topologies, module choices, deployment notes, and POC guidance.",
      "most_likely_effort": 8
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A9",
      "activity_name": "U8 - NYC taxi trip real-time analytics sample",
      "activity_description": "Build the DEBS 2015-style taxi trip analytics sample on Spring XD: HTTP ingestion of trip report CSV stream, parsing/enrichment modules (geo-grid mapping), sliding-window analytics streams (top routes, busy areas, revenue), results sink/output, sample data/scripts, lightweight performance sanity check, tests, and usage documentation.",
      "most_likely_effort": 15
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A10",
      "activity_name": "Cross-feature integration and system testing",
      "activity_description": "End-to-end testing across the increment: stream lifecycle with cleanup (U1/U2), edit-then-redeploy flows (U3), manifest-based deployment (U4), trace and side-channel behavior together (U6/U7) on RabbitMQ and Kafka, regression of existing stream deployment tests, and fixing defects found.",
      "most_likely_effort": 6
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A11",
      "activity_name": "Documentation and release packaging",
      "activity_description": "Update user reference docs and shell help for all new commands/properties, write release notes and migration notes (bus cleanup behavior, Kafka topic deletion caveats), package samples (U5/U8), and prepare the increment for release.",
      "most_likely_effort": 4
    }
  ],
  "reported_total": {
    "most_likely_effort": 79
  }
}

